# DISCLAIMER
This artifact is not intended for producation use. We recommend using this artifact in a sandboxed environment, such as the provided docker container.
By default, the aritfact can __not__ incur cost by quering a 3rd party LLM provider, however, if you configure the arifact with a LLM provider's API keys, you __will__ incur $ cost when running the artifact.

# Contents
This artifact contains the following:
- instructions for reproducing our results (in this README)
- a docker container with the runtime environment to run our code (vert.tar)
- the code used in our experiments (in the vert/ directory)
- the benchmark C, C++, and Go code we translated (in the vert/benchmarks/ directory)
- each benchmark directory contains the following:
	- the input source program, which is a .c/.cpp/.go file named after the benchmark
	- the logged LLM inputs and outputs in the prompt_log/ directory
	- the translation produced by our tool in the file translation/src/translation.rs

# Requirements
The only requirements for running our artifact is Docker.
It is also assumed you are using a unix environment.

# Setup
First, load the provided container with the command:
```bash
sudo docker pull aidanben/torust
```

Next, start up the docker container in an interactive shell.
You will need to mount the contents of this artifact to the /mnt folder in the container.
Run the following command from the some directory as this README.
```bash
sudo docker run -it -v $( pwd ):/mnt vert:latest
```
In the docker shell, navigate to the vert/ directory:
```bash
cd /mnt/vert
```
Next, test that translation is working by running the below command. The command below will run translation end-to-end, and will replay logged llm responses from the cache.

python3 torust.py --language c --benchmark-dir benchmark/c_transcoder/BASIC_AND_EXTENDED_EUCLIDEAN_ALGORITHMS --replay-cache

# Reproducing Our Results
This artifact can replicate the Claude-2 section of Table 1. It does not support the other LLMs (StarCoder, CodaLLaMA, and Transcoder-IR) reported in our paper out of the box. This is because these LLMs must be run locally and hence require significant compute resources. Typically, they require a hardware-specific implementation. However, our artifact can be extended to support these and orther LLMs with relative ease by extending the LLMQuery base class in llm.py. The Claude implementation can be used as a reference. The other LLMs can be found at the following links:
- https://github.com/facebookresearch/CodeGen/blob/main/docs/TransCoder-IR.md
- https://github.com/meta-llama/codellama
- https://github.com/bigcode-project/starcoder

The Claude-2 results can be reproduced without querying a real
LLM. This is accomplished by replaying the logged llm outputs included
in this artifact. Reproducing the entire Claude-2 section in Table 1
takes 40+ hours when running each experiment sequentially, so we
provide a reproducation script with a binary flag:
`--run-all`. Passing this flag reproduces the entire Claude-2 results,
and second reproduces a subset of our benchmarks. You can run the
small script as shown below. The results are printed to the terminal.

```bash
python test_eval.py            # subset replication
python test_eval.py  --run-all # multi-day multi-100GB full replication
```

This script will take 1-2 hours to complete and requires:
- 1GB of free disk space
- Ideally 4 CPU free cores
- 8GB RAM

While running experiments can be done in parallel, we do not recommend
doing so unless you have substantial compute and free disk
space. Compiled Rust binaries take a significant amount of space (100+
MB each), and translation produces multuple binaries. Running a single
benchmark produces 500+ MB of compiled Rust binaries.

Running this script should produce the following `result.json` files
at each benchmark directory.

```json
{
        "project": "BENCHMARK_NAME",
        "compile": true,
        "bolero": false,
        "bounded_kani": false
}
```

You can aggregate the results from the JSON files:
- To count the number of C programs that passed bolero (Table 1 column "PBT")
```bash
find benchmark/c_transcoder/ -type f -name result.json -exec cat {} \; | grep bolero | grep true | wc -l
```
- `grep compile` and `grep bounded_kani` should be used for "Compiled" and "BMC" column.
- For other benchmarks, swap `c_transcoder` for the respective library
- `grep true | wc -l` should be retained for all variants.

